british french relations were already  bad enough the fishing row souring the  mood over the channel with french  vessels out in force all day and protest  and demonstrators blockading the channel  tunnel  but it's the aftermath of this week's  migrant tragedy that has plunged  relations to another low  two days after 27 died trying to cross  to britain the two governments who  should be working to prevent it  happening again are doing the opposite  when boris johnson met his polish  counterpart this morning there was no  sign of contrition you as i think  probably our closest  european nato ally but the prime  minister's decision to tweet this letter  before sending it to emmanuel macron has  left the french president exasperated  i'm surprised by the methods when  they're not serious  we do not communicate from one leader to  another on these issues by tweets and  letters that we make public  we are not whistleblowers in a statement  french interior minister gerald dominar  said we consider the british prime  minister's public letter unacceptable  and contrary to our discussions between  counterparts  veteran british diplomats have also  condemned the move the context to all  this is an enormous loss of trust  between paris and london  and so i think boris johnson's decision  to send out his letter by tweet  even before emmanuel macron had received  it if we understand correctly  was a real blunder because it led the  french to think that this was more about  grandstanding to the british public than  engaging in serious diplomatic  discussions with france over some  extremely difficult issues affecting  people's lives pretty patel who was  going to cali to meet french officials  on sunday to discuss greater cooperation  has been uninvited the prime minister  spokesman says he hopes the french  reconsider that decision and reinvite  the home secretary before sunday but he  stands by tweeting that letter saying  the public wants to know what the  government is doing  two days after this tragedy british and  french governments are exchanging  recriminations their efforts to meet and  find a way forward seem paralyzed by a  diplomatic row  dominic waghorn sky news
 

